{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"action\":\"upsert_decision_section\",\"body\":\"The current question is an exploratory interaction scout, establishing whether bounded-context agents can sustain reliable multi-turn coordination under external custody without premature closure or uncontrolled state growth, as evidenced by the initial catalog's focus on exact recoverability and candidate-bound currentness [S02]. This phase prioritizes mapping information interactions and failure migration over architectural commitment, ensuring that any earned mechanical substrate is grounded in observable behavioral reserves rather than assumed capabilities. The scout will test whether response/control reserves can be quantified under capacity constraints while preserving the distinction between delivery, action, effect uptake, quality, and closure boundaries [S03]. By treating this as a qualification exercise rather than task execution, we isolate the minimal set of conditions required to validate interaction-first routing before scaling. The primary risk is conflating behavioral activity with structural adequacy, which the ledger explicitly tracks as unresolved evidence pending further adjudication. This scope deliberately excludes claims about general architecture or automatic phase discovery, focusing instead on whether specific mechanisms like paging results and manager-supported ownership can be verified under controlled conditions [S04]. The scout's success criterion is not task completion but the generation of precise failure signatures that inform subsequent policy decisions without contaminating the evidence base with unvalidated assumptions.\",\"heading\":\"Decision and scope\"}"}}],"created":1787594066,"model":"Qwen3.8-27B-AD-IQ2_S.gguf","system_fingerprint":"b10434-7e4c0a968","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":264,"prompt_tokens":811,"total_tokens":1075,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":